# Joints and couplings: a first declarative mechanics layer

Status: design note, **not ratified, not implemented, not a public API
promise**. Date: 2026-09-09. Written after the pilot reviewed
`mechanics-ontology.md` against wall clock 01 and Thor and set its class
catalogue aside. This note records the direction settled in that discussion
and the first layer proposed under it, for revision by the pilot. No project
or framework implementation is changed by this document.

## The decision behind it

`mechanics-ontology.md` grew from a survey of 29 project integrations. A
survey produces a row per thing seen, and its rows became classes: seventeen
submodules and some sixty names. Applied to clock 01, its own step one needed
typed interface descriptors, a joint family, an angle-constant change to the
dimension algebra and a network compiler, to remove about 150 lines of clock
motion code. The pilot's objection: that trades one obfuscation, lots of
code, for another, lots of things to learn. What made drivers and ports
expressive was not vocabulary but one primitive with one verb.

The history of mechanism theory ran this experiment already. Reuleaux
(*Theoretische Kinematik*, 1875) decomposed every machine into *kinematic
pairs* closed into chains: six lower pairs with surface contact (revolute,
prismatic, screw, cylindrical, spherical, planar) and higher pairs with line
or point contact (gear teeth, cams, rolling wheels). That part survived and
was formalized: Hervé (1978) listed the subgroups of the rigid-motion group
SE(3), and the lower pairs are exactly their mechanical generators. On top of
the pairs Reuleaux also built a symbol notation and a classification of
mechanisms, the direction that Artobolevsky's five-volume catalogue later
took to its end. Moon's history records that "Reuleaux's symbol notation all
but died with his passing"; nobody builds on the classification. Every
simulation format in use today is the pairs plus one more thing, a coupling
between two joint coordinates:

| System | Pairs | Couplings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| MuJoCo | `hinge`, `slide`, `ball`, `free` joints inside the body tree | `equality/joint` with `polycoef`; `tendon/fixed` as a linear combination of joints |
| Modelica MultiBody | `Joints.Revolute`, `Joints.Prismatic`, spherical, universal, planar | `Rotational.IdealGear`, `Joints.GearConstraint`, loop constraints |
| STEP AP242 kinematics | revolute, prismatic, cylindrical, screw, spherical, planar, universal pairs | gear and rack-and-pinion as low-order pairs "with motion coupling"; point-on-curve and point-on-surface for cams |
| URDF / SDF | joint types | `mimic` (multiplier, offset); `gearbox` |

The pilot's intention is that solid-node's mechanics layer fits MuJoCo and
Modelica. So the layer is Reuleaux's two categories, stated the way those
systems state them: a **joint** that is a pair, and a **coupling** that
relates two joint coordinates. Not a catalogue of mechanisms.

The shop's `docs/mujoco-viability.md` (2026-09-06) reached the same first
step from the other side: there is no joint topology to export from a
solid-node tree today, because a revolute joint is "an emergent property of
the author having written `self.rotate(self.angle, [0, 0, 1])`", and its
recommendation was "declared joints first".

## The layer: three concepts, one verb

### 1. A joint is a port that knows what it moves

Two kinds to start, the two one-coordinate lower pairs both targets have
natively:

```python
elbow = Revolute(axis=(0, 1, 0), at=(0, 0, 81.5), range=(-135, 135), unit='deg')
carriage = Prismatic(axis=(1, 0, 0), range=(0, 200), unit='mm')
```

A joint is declared on the node it moves. Its axis and anchor are stated in
the parent's frame, where MuJoCo puts a hinge's `axis` and `pos` and where a
Modelica Revolute sits between two frames. Binding the joint moves the body.
The rest placement a project already applies in `render()` stays as it is;
the framework composes the joint motion about the anchor on top of it,
carrying the axis and pivot into the part's own frame. That is the arithmetic
Thor's `placing.py` does by hand (`rotate_about`, `into_local`, `turn_about`,
`axis_sign`), and it leaves the project.

A joint is a port. It binds in `simulate()` like any port, it can be passed
to children as a token so several leaves share one coordinate, and a
`Driver` at the root is what an independent joint reads. The screw pair
comes later as a revolute with a pitch; neither target has a native screw
joint and both build it from hinge plus slide plus a coupling.

### 2. A coupling is `a.drives(b)`

Written in a class body, on the declaration on the left, between two joint
coordinates:

```python
power.drives(centre)
centre.turn.drives(motion_works.cannon.turn, offset=hand_setting)
elbow_pulley.turn.drives(elbow_belt.travel, ratio=pitch_arc(117))
```

- **The law belongs to the class on the left, not to the verb.** `drives` is
  the only word. What "an arbor drives an arbor" means is a method on the
  project's `Arbor`: the tooth ratio, the sign of an external pair, the
  registration. What "the anchor drives the escape wheel" means is the
  project's escapement law. A class that says nothing gets the default, an
  affine relation with `ratio` and `offset`. The framework holds no list of
  shapes; a class that drives in a way nobody has written yet is written in
  the project and moves into the framework only when a second project writes
  it. The existing `solid_node.mechanisms` functions (`meshed_angle`,
  `screw_travel`, ...) remain available as ways to compute a ratio and
  offset; they are not vocabulary.
- **Direction is mechanical; solving is not.** The sentence states which way
  power goes. At realization the framework finds which coordinates are
  bound, by a driver or by `simulate()`, and orients each relation from the
  known side. An affine law inverts itself, so a clock solved backwards from
  its escapement costs the author nothing. A law that cannot be inverted is
  refused by name at realization, not turned into a pose.
- **A relation may name a declaration by path.** `art5.drives(shoulder.art2.art3.art4.art56.wrist)`
  from the root, so a driver reaches a deep joint without every intermediate
  node redeclaring and forwarding the port. The tree of parts stays the tree
  of parts; the drive network is laid over it.
- **Naming is optional.** `great_pair = power.drives(centre)` when a test
  wants to reach the relation; a bare statement otherwise.

Every affine coupling lowers to the same expression math the viewer already
evaluates (ADR-076), so no new cross-runtime surface is needed.

### 3. A derived coordinate is a formula over joints

```python
relative_elbow = art3.elbow - shoulder      # the elbow belt is anchored below the shoulder
left_pinion = wrist + 2 * tool              # a bevel differential, seen from outside
```

Same mechanism as derived parameters, over ports instead of tokens. Thor's
elbow belt and wrist differential need it, and it is the one concept here
that is not a Reuleaux pair. In MuJoCo a linear one is a `tendon/fixed`.

## Fit with the targets

| solid-node | MuJoCo | Modelica MultiBody |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Revolute`, `Prismatic` with axis, anchor, range | `joint type="hinge"` / `"slide"` with `axis`, `pos`, `range`, inside the body | `Joints.Revolute(n=axis)` / `Joints.Prismatic` between frames, with limits |
| `a.drives(b)` affine | `equality/joint` with `polycoef` (linear case) | `Rotational.IdealGear` ratio, or `Joints.GearConstraint` |
| derived coordinate, linear | `tendon/fixed` | a sum of gear couplings |
| `a.drives(b, law=...)` non-polynomial | no constraint form; the escapement would be real contact | no ideal element; a custom equation |
| `Driver` | `actuator` on the joint | an input signal on the joint |
| tree of nodes with rest placements | body tree with `pos`/`quat` and mesh geoms | `FixedTranslation` and `Body` per part |

The fourth row is the honesty line. A prescribed law is kinematic-only; an
exporter must report it rather than emit nothing silently. The ontology
document's distinction between geometry-derived, prescribed and approximated
behaviour survives as that label.

## What it does to wall clock 01

Illustrative, with the proposed names. The leaves still come from the
`clocks` library through the built movement; only how they move changes.

```python
# ---- shared/parts.py: the leaves that carry a law ----------------------

class Arbor(CadQueryNode):
    '''A printed wheel and pinion, one body, two toothed faces.'''
    index = Count(min=0)
    #: read off the built movement at realization, like the shape is
    wheel_teeth = Count(...)
    pinion_teeth = Count(...)
    wheel_gap = Angle(...)        # where MrBunsy's Gear puts a gap at zero
    pinion_tip = Angle(...)       # and a tooth tip

    def drives(self, other: 'Arbor'):
        '''In a going train my wheel always drives the next arbor's pinion:
        an external pair, so the sense reverses.'''
        return self.affine(other,
                           ratio=-self.wheel_teeth / other.pinion_teeth,
                           offset=registration(self.wheel_gap, other.pinion_tip))


class MotionWorksPart(Arbor):
    def drives(self, other):
        '''Here it is the pinion that drives the wheel.'''
        return self.affine(other,
                           ratio=-self.pinion_teeth / other.wheel_teeth,
                           offset=registration(...))


# ---- shared/assemblies.py -------------------------------------------------

class TrainArbor(AssemblyNode):
    '''One arbor: what turns together at one bearing.'''
    index = Count(min=0)
    #: about the plates' z, at the bearing the plates give this index;
    #: `at` is in the parent's frame, so the render() translate goes
    turn = Revolute(axis=Z, at=bearing_of(index), unit='deg')

    wheel = Arbor(index=index, turn=turn)      # the port passed down: one coordinate
        rod = ArborRod(index=index, turn=turn)


class MotionWorks(AssemblyNode):
    cannon = MotionWorksPart(index=0)
    arbor = MotionWorksPart(index=1)
    hour_holder = MotionWorksPart(index=2)
    minute_hand = Hand(index=0)
    hour_hand = Hand(index=1)

    cannon.drives(arbor)
    arbor.drives(hour_holder)
    cannon.drives(minute_hand)                 # pushed on: ratio 1, offset 0
    hour_holder.drives(hour_hand)


class Pendulum(AssemblyNode):
    swing = Revolute(axis=Z, unit='deg')       # at the anchor's bearing, see Movement
    holder = PendulumHolder(turn=swing)
    rod = PendulumRod(turn=swing)
    bob = Bob(turn=swing)


class Movement(AssemblyNode):
    seconds = SignalPort(unit='s')

    power = TrainArbor(index=0)
    centre = TrainArbor(index=1)
    third = TrainArbor(index=2)
    escape = TrainArbor(index=3)
    anchor = TrainArbor(index=4)
    pendulum = Pendulum()
    motion_works = MotionWorks()

    # the going train, in the direction the power goes
    power.drives(centre)
    centre.drives(third)
    third.drives(escape)

    # the anchor's tail carries the pendulum; the cannon pinion is a
    # friction fit on the minute arbor, set once when the hands go on
    anchor.turn.drives(pendulum.swing)
    centre.turn.drives(motion_works.cannon.turn, offset=hand_setting)

    def simulate(self):
        # The one prescribed law in the clock, and the only line of
        # motion code left: where the escapement holds the wheel and
        # where it has swung the anchor to, at this second. Everything
        # else above is solved from these two, backwards through the
        # train to the great wheel and forwards into the hands.
        t = self.seconds.value
        self.escape.turn = escape_wheel_angle(t, self.built.escapement, ...)
        self.anchor.turn = anchor_angle(t, self.built.escapement, ...)


class WallClock01(AssemblyNode):
    time = Time(loop=TIMELINE)
    plates = Frame(index=0)
    standoffs = Frame(index=1)
    movement = Movement(seconds=time)

    def render(self):
        for part in children_of(self):
            part.rotate(UPRIGHT, [1, 0, 0])
            part.rotate(self.facing, Z)
```

Reading down: each arbor is one revolute pair about the plates' z at its
bearing, and the printed wheel and the steel rod both take that one
coordinate. The train is three sentences, and the law for "an arbor drives an
arbor" is written once on the project's `Arbor`, with the tooth counts and
the registration it already reads off the library. The motion works is four
sentences with its own law, because there the pinion drives the wheel. The
pendulum is a second revolute on the anchor's coordinate. The escapement
stays exactly the code it is today, bound in `simulate()`, visibly the only
place a law is written rather than declared.

Gone from the project: `Movement.arbor_angles`, `motion_works_angles`,
`gear_pair`, `line_of_centres`, the two wrappers in `depthing.py`, the
`simulate()` of `TrainArbor`, `MotionWorks`, `Hands` and `Pendulum`, the
`simulate_for_seconds` fan-out, and the bearing translate in
`TrainArbor.render()`. Colours and the library build are untouched.

At realization the framework resolves the declarations to bound joints,
notices that `escape.turn` and `anchor.turn` are bound by `simulate()` and
`seconds` by the root, orients `third.drives(escape)` backwards, then
`centre.drives(third)`, then `power.drives(centre)`, and carries
`centre.turn` forward through the cannon offset into the hands. The existing
test that six hours turns the hour hand half way round becomes the only place
the twelve-to-one is stated: change a tooth count and the test, not the model,
notices.

## What it does to Thor

Thor has no central angle function; it has seven root drivers forwarded down
five levels of ports, and at each level a `simulate()` that turns a child
about an axis, spins gears and motors at ratios and feeds belts. Against the
layer:

- **Fits directly.** Every transmission is affine: base pinion in the base
  ring (10:50), two shoulder pinions in the upper arm's internal ring (10:60),
  each pinion to its motor (1:1), the elbow belt's drive pulley, optical disc
  and motor (117:20), the forearm yaw pinion (-20:10) and the slewing race's
  ball cage (1/2), the wrist pinions and pulleys at twice the tool roll in
  opposite senses with motors at 2:1 behind them, and the belts' molejo
  `travel` ports taking the pulley arc. The differential needs no class: left
  is wrist plus twice tool, right is wrist minus twice tool.
- **Needs the joint.** The elbow axis is 81.5 mm from the forearm's origin,
  the wrist axis 111.5 mm up the forearm, and half the parts come out of
  FreeCAD facing the other way. `Revolute(axis=..., at=...)` in the parent
  frame is what removes `placing.py`.
- **Needs derived coordinates.** `elbow - shoulder` because the elbow belt is
  anchored on the shoulder housing; the differential sums above.
- **Needs relations by path.** Thirty lines of `self.art3.wrist = self.wrist`
  and five nodes declaring `yaw, wrist, tool, grip` only to forward them.
- **Direction reads better than in the clock.** `shoulder_motor.drives(upper_arm, ratio=1/6)`
  is the physically true sentence; the value still flows from the `art2`
  driver backwards. The clock states the kinematic direction because its
  escapement law does not invert; Thor's laws all do.

A sample, the upper arm and the shoulder housing:

```python
class Art2(AssemblyNode):
    shoulder = Revolute(axis=(0, 0, 1), range=SHOULDER_TRAVEL, unit='deg')
    art3 = Art3()
    elbow_pulley = Pulley(teeth=117)
    elbow_belt = flexibles.ElbowBelt()
    relative_elbow = art3.elbow - shoulder
    relative_elbow.drives(elbow_pulley.turn)
    elbow_pulley.turn.drives(elbow_belt.travel, ratio=pitch_arc(117))


class Art1(AssemblyNode):
    art2 = Art2()
    shoulder_pinion_1 = Gear(teeth=10)
    shoulder_motor_1 = Motor()
    art2.shoulder.drives(shoulder_pinion_1.turn, ratio=60 / 10)   # internal ring, same sense
    shoulder_pinion_1.turn.drives(shoulder_motor_1.spin)
```

The awkward case: each gripper jaw is the coupler of a parallelogram. It
translates on a circle and never turns, which is not one coordinate driven by
one law. Kinematically it can be two prismatic joints each driven by a law
of the grip opening, labelled prescribed. Physically it is a closed loop,
which is `equality/connect` in MuJoCo and a loop constraint in Modelica, and
that is outside this layer.

Roughly, Thor's motion code is two hundred lines across six `simulate()`
methods and the placing helpers. It becomes a few dozen sentences, and the
placing helpers leave the project. Parts, design placements, fasteners and
belts are untouched.

## Framework surface, and the records it touches

- **Declarative nodes.** The spec refuses reading an attribute off a child
  declaration in a class body (scenario "A sideways read is refused"). This
  layer needs a narrow relaxation: reading a *port* off a declaration, at any
  depth, yields a path reference resolved per parent instance at realization.
  Reading a *parameter* stays refused. ADR-061 is extended, not reversed.
- **Ports.** `Revolute` and `Prismatic` are port subclasses carrying axis,
  anchor and range; binding applies the motion. Ports may be passed to child
  declarations as tokens are. ADR-056's deferred flow variable is untouched,
  and nothing here precludes it.
- **Declaration arguments resolved against the instance.** Clock 01's tooth
  counts and bearing positions come out of the library's ratio search on the
  built movement, not from literal tokens. Joint and coupling arguments must
  resolve at realization against the instance, the way child arguments do.
- **Mechanisms.** No new module. The existing laws stay as functions. The
  degree-literal question ADR-076 left open (an `Angle` constant in the
  algebra) is not required by this layer because joint values are port
  values, not declared tokens; it is still open.
- **Network resolution, private.** Inventory bound coordinates, orient each
  relation, lower to expressions, and raise on a coordinate no relation or
  binding reaches, on two bindings of one coordinate, and on a
  non-invertible law needed backwards. Errors name the node paths and the
  relation.

## Acceptance

1. Clock 01 and Thor render, pass their existing suites, and their motion
   code shrinks as described. Pixels remain evidence.
2. Changing a tooth count in clock 01 propagates to the hands with no other
   edit, and the six-hour test fails when the twelve-to-one is broken.
3. A coordinate nothing reaches, a doubly bound coordinate, and a
   non-invertible law needed backwards each fail by name at realization.
4. A walk of the realized tree builds a `mujoco.MjSpec`: bodies with mesh
   geoms, hinges and slides with axis, position and range, `equality/joint`
   and `tendon/fixed` for the couplings, actuators for the drivers, and
   compiles with contacts disabled for the clock train and for Thor.
   Prescribed laws are reported, not silently dropped. This proves the layer
   carries what MuJoCo needs before any dynamics question is asked. The
   Modelica emission is the same walk producing text, checked for shape
   until OpenModelica is available.

## Deliberately out

Closed loops and their solver (Grübler mobility, the gripper), stateful
mechanisms (ratchets, engagement, neutral), the screw pair, mass and density,
non-holonomic contact (a wheel on ground), and any flow variable on ports.
Each has a place to go later without reshaping the three concepts above, and
each is named as an exclusion in the ports spec, ADR-056 or
`docs/mujoco-viability.md` already.

## Relationship to `mechanics-ontology.md`

That document stays as the coverage survey it is: evidence of which kinds of
motion the local catalogue contains, and a checklist the primitive must
eventually satisfy. Its class catalogue is not the plan. Its sufficiency
order (clock 01 first, then compound and reversible relations, then loops and
multi-input constraints, then orbit versus spin, then state) still reads as a
sensible order of fixtures.

